INT. ROADSIDE MOTEL - ROOM 9 - NIGHT

Pink bedspread. Wood-paneled walls. A TV bolted to the dresser, off. A lamp with a torn shade. Rain taps the window like fingertips.

A WOMAN, 30s, wet raincoat, dark circles under her eyes, locks the door. Slides the chain. Pulls the curtains shut. She is alone, and she's checking the room the way a person checks who has already been surprised once.

She opens the closet. Empty hangers. The bathroom. Empty tub. Under the bed. Dust, a lost sock.

She sits on the edge of the bed and takes a small tape recorder from her bag. She presses RECORD.

WOMAN
(into recorder)
Test. Test.

She stops. Plays it back. Her own voice, tinny.

WOMAN (V.O.)
(tape)
Test. Test.

She nods. Records again.

WOMAN
Day one. Motel off Route 9. Nobody followed me. I think.

She stops the tape. Looks at the phone on the nightstand. Beige, rotary, curly cord. She picks up the receiver. Dial tone. She sets it back.

She rewinds. Hits PLAY.

WOMAN (V.O.)
(tape)
Day one. Motel off Route 9. Nobody followed me. I think.

Then, after her recorded voice, the same voice again, lower, almost tender, saying words she never spoke:

WOMAN (V.O.)
(tape, whispering)
I'm in the room.

She freezes. The tape continues to hiss.

She hits STOP. Her thumb shakes. She rewinds again, plays it again. The extra line is gone. Only the first.

WOMAN
(quietly)
Okay.

She sets the recorder on the dresser. Takes off her coat. Hangs it on the doorknob. A key tag drops from the pocket and clatters to the floor. She picks it up. The tag is stamped in hand-scratched letters: PROPERTY OF LENA MARSH.

She turns it over, turns it back.

LENA
(to herself)
Right. Right. I'm Lena.

She says it as if reminding herself. She says it again.

LENA (CONT'D)
Lena Marsh. Thirty-four. Dental hygienist. Born in Duluth.

She reaches for the lamp. Clicks it off. The room is almost black. A glow leaks around the curtain edge.

On the dresser, the recorder clicks on by itself. The tape reels turn.

LENA (CONT'D)
No.

LENA (V.O.)
(tape)
Lena.

She backs against the wall.

LENA
That's my voice.

LENA (V.O.)
(tape)
You taped me yesterday. In the car.

LENA
I wasn't in a car yesterday.

LENA (V.O.)
(tape)
No. But you will be.

She lunges. Grabs the recorder. Rips the cassette out. Throws it across the room. It hits the TV and cracks.

Silence. Rain. Breath.

She slides down the wall until she's sitting on the floor. She hugs her knees.

From the bathroom: a click. A drip. Another click.

LENA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Please.

The bathroom light snaps on by itself. A thin line of yellow slides under the door.

LENA (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Please don't.

The doorknob turns on its own.

She crawls to the bed. Under it. Dust, the lost sock. Her own hand finds the cracked cassette.

The bathroom door creaks.

LENA (V.O.)
(from the cracked cassette, gentle)
Day two.

She presses the cassette to her chest. Doesn't breathe.

The bathroom light hums. The cassette hisses against her chest.

LENA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Nobody followed me.

LENA
(a thread of voice)
I know.

The lamp clicks on.
